course: chemistry
lesson: 25
title: Cleaning chemicals and safety at home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Open the cupboard under the kitchen sink and you will find bottles of real, strong chemicals: bleach, toilet cleaner, oven spray, dishwasher tablets. They are strong on purpose, so that they can break down dirt, grease and germs.
explain: The same strength that breaks down dirt can burn skin, hurt eyes and poison a body, and a small child's body most of all. So these bottles carry warning pictures, tricky caps and the words "keep out of reach of children".
explain: Some cleaners must never meet: bleach poured together with an acid toilet cleaner gives off chlorine, a poisonous gas. The safe rule is simple: never mix cleaning products at all, and leave them to grown-ups.
explain: Chemicals stay in their own bottles, sprays are used with a window open, and if anything splashes into an eye it is rinsed with lots of clean water straight away while someone tells an adult.
fact: Bleach kills germs and takes out stains; it is a strong alkali and can burn skin and eyes.
fact: Many toilet cleaners are acids, and mixing an acid with bleach gives off chlorine, a poisonous gas; mixing bleach with ammonia cleaners makes other harmful gases.
fact: The safe rule is never to mix any cleaning products together.
fact: Warning symbols are black pictures inside red-bordered diamonds; they mean the same in many countries, so people understand them without reading.
fact: The corrosive symbol shows a liquid eating into a hand and a metal bar; other symbols warn of fire, poison and harm to nature.
fact: Child-resistant caps need a push and a twist at the same time, which is hard for small hands; they slow a child down but are not a lock.
fact: Brightly coloured liquid capsules for washing machines have been mistaken for sweets by small children.
fact: Sprays put droplets and fumes into the air you breathe, so labels say to open a window or use them in a well-aired room.
fact: Chemicals must never be poured into drinks bottles; people have drunk them by mistake from juice and water bottles.
fact: A smaller body is harmed by a smaller amount, so medicines and chemicals that are fine for adults can badly hurt a child.
fact: A splash in the eye is rinsed with lots of clean running water for many minutes while an adult is told; if something is swallowed, adults call a doctor or the emergency number with the bottle to hand.
fact: Goggles are worn in the school lab because eyes are delicate and some chemicals damage them in seconds.
fact: A swallowed button battery can burn through the inside of a child's throat within hours, so it is an emergency: go to hospital at once.
exercise: Why is bleach kept on a high shelf or in a locked cupboard? | It can burn skin and eyes and is poisonous | It goes off quickly in the dark | It loses its smell when kept low | It is too heavy for small children to lift
exercise: Why must bleach never be mixed with an acid toilet cleaner? | Together they give off a poisonous gas | Together they stop cleaning anything at all | Together they turn into plain water | Together they leave a sticky stain
exercise: What is the safest rule about mixing cleaning products? | Never mix any of them together | Only mix them in a big plastic bucket | Only mix ones of the same colour | Mix them only with warm water first
exercise: Why are warning symbols pictures rather than words? | People can understand them without reading | Pictures are much cheaper to print than words | Words would wash off in the bath | Pictures are only for decoration
exercise: What does the symbol of a liquid eating into a hand and a metal bar mean? | Corrosive: it can burn skin and eat metal | Flammable: it catches fire very easily indeed | Harmful to fish and water plants | Keep the bottle in the fridge
exercise: How does a child-resistant cap work? | It needs a push and a twist at the same time | It only opens with a special key from the shop | It opens only when the bottle is warm | It locks itself after one use
exercise: Why are washing-machine capsules a danger to small children? | They are bright and squishy, like sweets | They explode with a bang if they get wet | They give off gas in the cupboard | They are too heavy to pick up
exercise: Why does the label on a bathroom spray say to open a window? | The spray puts fumes into the air you breathe | The spray works only in fresh cold air from outside | The spray turns into water in the dark | The window lets the smell of lemons in
exercise: Why should cleaner never be poured into an old juice bottle? | Someone could drink it thinking it is juice | The juice bottle would melt at once | The cleaner stops working in plastic | The cleaner turns into juice over time
exercise: Why is a small amount of medicine more dangerous for a toddler than for an adult? | A smaller body is harmed by a smaller amount | Toddlers swallow everything much faster than adults | Medicine is stronger in the morning | Toddlers do not have a stomach yet
exercise: Some cleaner splashes into your eye. What should you do first? | Rinse it with lots of clean water and tell an adult | Rub it hard with your sleeve to dry it | Close it tight and wait for it to stop | Put a drop of milk in it and lie down
exercise: Why do pupils wear goggles in the school lab? | Eyes are delicate and splashes harm them fast | Goggles help you see small things much bigger | Goggles keep the room smells out | The teacher can see who is working
exercise: A toddler may have swallowed a button battery. What should happen? | Go to hospital at once, it is an emergency | Wait a day or two to see if it comes out by itself | Give the toddler a glass of fizzy drink | Nothing, button batteries are harmless
exercise: Why is chlorine gas from mixed cleaners dangerous? | It is poisonous to breathe | It makes the room much too cold | It makes the bathroom very dark | It sets off every smoke alarm
exercise: What do the words "keep out of reach of children" tell you? | The product can hurt children even in small amounts | The product is only for children over five | The product works best on high shelves | The product must be used by children only
exercise: Someone has swallowed a cleaning product. What do the adults have ready when they call for help? | The bottle with its label | A big glass of salty water | A written list of the house rules | The paper receipt from the shop
test: Jake: "toilet's well grim, chuck bleach AND the toilet stuff in, double power innit". What's true? | No, the mix gives off a poisonous gas | Yes, two cleaners together clean twice as well | Yes, but only if you use very hot water | No, but only because it wastes your money
test: Your cousin keeps the plant spray in an old lemonade bottle "cos it's handy". What is the danger? | Someone may drink it thinking it's lemonade | The bottle will slowly dissolve from the inside | The spray will turn into lemonade | There is no danger at all if the lid is on
test: "it's got one of them child caps so the baby's fine with it". Right? | No, a cap only slows a child down | Yes, babies can never open them at all | Yes, the caps are completely locked | No, the caps are easy for babies to bite
test: A cartoon scientist splashes potion in his eye and just blinks and carries on. What should really happen? | Rinse with lots of clean water and get help | Blink fast until the eye stops stinging | Rub the eye until the potion is gone | Cover the eye and keep on working
test: "shut up about goggles, it's only water in the beaker". Why wear them anyway? | You can't always be sure what is in a beaker | Water is the most dangerous liquid of them all | Goggles stop water from boiling over | The rules are only there to annoy you
test: If a cleaning spray is used in a tiny bathroom with the door and window shut, what happens? | Fumes build up in the air you breathe | The spray cleans twice as well | The fumes turn back into liquid | The spray goes off and stops working
test: Little sister found a shiny colourful capsule in the laundry box: "sweetie!" What should you do? | Take it away gently and tell an adult | Let her try it, a little is harmless | Rinse it under the tap and give it back | Hide it in your pocket and say nothing
test: Which of these is safe? | Keeping cleaners in their own labelled bottles | Mixing two cleaners to make a stronger, better one | Putting bleach in a cup to carry it | Storing tablets in a sweet jar
test: Mum dropped the TV remote and a button battery rolled away, the toddler's been near it. "crap, can't find it". What's the right move? | Treat it as an emergency and get help now | Wait and see if he gets a tummy ache | Let him drink water and forget it | Look again tomorrow when it is light
test: Why might a few vitamin pills that look like sweets hurt a small child? | A small body is harmed by a smaller amount | Vitamins are always a poison for everyone who takes them | Sweet-looking pills are made of plastic | Children cannot take in any vitamins
test: The red-bordered diamond with a flame on a can of spray tells you what? | It catches fire easily | It is perfectly safe to drink | It is good for the environment | It must be kept warm and dry
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the admixture of bleach with an acidic lavatory cleaner is expressly prohibited. | The admixture liberates a poisonous gas | The admixture neutralises all cleaning action | The admixture generates excessive foam | The admixture produces an unpleasant colour
exam: In the event of a chemical substance entering the eye, which course of action is most consistent with established first-aid guidance? | Copious rinsing with clean water and notification of an adult | Vigorous rubbing of the eye with a dry cloth | Closure of the eye pending spontaneous recovery | Application of a household cleaner to neutralise the substance within the eye
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a child-resistant closure? | It impedes, but does not wholly prevent, access by young children | It renders the contents entirely inaccessible to all persons | It preserves the contents from deterioration by light | It indicates that the contents are suitable for children
exam: It is given that a young child is suspected of having ingested a button battery. Select the response that is consistent with the established facts. | Immediate attendance at hospital is required | Observation for several days is sufficient | The battery is harmless once swallowed | A carbonated drink should be administered
